-- title:
--   Pure braids factor through the forgetful kernel and a section
-- statement:
--   The pure braid group on $n+1$ strands is generated by the kernel of the forgetful map together with the image of a section of it:
--
--   $$P_{n+1} \;=\; \ker\bigl(p_* : P_{n+1} \to P_n\bigr) \;\vee\; \operatorname{im}(s_*),$$
--
--   where $s$ is a homomorphic section, $p_* \circ s_* = \mathrm{id}$.
--
--   This is the group-theoretic skeleton of Artin combing. The classical normal form writes a pure braid as a product of a "free part", lying in the kernel of the map that forgets the last strand, and a pure braid on one fewer strand, reinserted by the section. The statement here is exactly that such a factorisation always exists, with no claim about uniqueness and no identification of the kernel.
--
--   Two ingredients. The existence of the section is `BraidsLinksMCG.pureBraid_forget_section`, which is proved: one adjoins to a configuration the point
--
--   $$z(p) \;=\; (n+1) + \sum_i \max\bigl(\operatorname{Re}p_i - n,\ 0\bigr),$$
--
--   lying strictly to the right of every existing point and equal to $n+1$ on the base configuration, so the construction is continuous and basepoint-preserving. The rest is the general fact that a split surjection of groups is generated by its kernel and the image of the section: given $x$, the element $x \cdot s_*(p_*(x))^{-1}$ lies in the kernel because $p_* \circ s_* = \mathrm{id}$, and $x$ is its product with $s_*(p_*(x))$.
--
--   What this deliberately does not say. It does not identify $\ker p_*$ with a free group. That identification is the content of the Fadell--Neuwirth exact sequence together with the computation of $\pi_1$ of a punctured plane, and is tracked separately as `BraidsLinksMCG.fadellNeuwirth_ker_le_range`, `BraidsLinksMCG.fadellNeuwirth_incl_injective` and `BraidsLinksMCG.puncturedPlaneGroup_equiv_free`. Nor does it assert that the product is semidirect in the sense of a normal complement, though the kernel is of course normal; the join of subgroups is all that is claimed.
--
--   The value of separating it out is that the splitting half of the combing argument turns out to be elementary and available now, while the kernel half is not. Anyone setting up an induction on strand count --- for the generation of the braid group by half-twists, for faithfulness of the Artin representation, or for the computation of the centre --- needs this factorisation as the inductive step, and can take it as given rather than rebuilding it alongside the harder topology.
-- source:
--   Birman, Braids, Links and Mapping Class Groups, Chapter 1, Corollary 1.8.1 and the combing normal form; Artin, Theory of braids, Ann. of Math. 48 (1947).

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BraidsLinksMCG_ConfigSpace

namespace BraidsLinksMCG

theorem pureBraid_ker_sup_section_eq_top (n : ℕ) :
    ∃ s : PureBraidGroup n →* PureBraidGroup (n + 1),
      (FundamentalGroup.mapOfEq (configForget n) (configForget_base n)).comp s =
          MonoidHom.id (PureBraidGroup n) ∧
        (FundamentalGroup.mapOfEq (configForget n) (configForget_base n)).ker ⊔ s.range = ⊤ := by
  sorry

end BraidsLinksMCG
